<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>
  <!--
    The C-27J Spartan, our first aeroplane (docs/development/vehicle-c27j.md, slice 2 of its "Order of work"; MV7 in
    modern-military-vehicles.md, "the rough-strip transport aeroplane"). It keeps its real name (that record's decision 12). Its figures are
    the record's: real kilograms and kilowatts, marked there [P] primary, [S] secondary, [I] inferred, [A] authored. It never hovers: it
    flies on our own flight as an aeroplane (Patches/C27J/Flight_C27J.xml), and carries a vehicle in its hold and drops by parachute
    (Patches/C27J/Airdrop_C27J.xml). Not yet tested in game.

    Footprint 5 x 11 (owner's card of 26 Nov 2026: the Osprey's scale): cells x -2 to 2, z -5 (tail) to 5 (nose). The picture is 34 x 27
    cells (28.7 m span and 22.7 m length [S] at the Osprey's 1.175 cells a metre); the wings and nacelles reach far past the footprint, so
    their parts are laid on its outer columns, one outer part to a cell (vehicle-armour.md): the cockpit across the two nose rows, the hold
    down the middle in three parts, the wings and nacelles on the outer columns of rows 3 to 1, the main gear in the sponsons beside the
    centre hold, the ramp across rows -2 and -3, the tail across the last two. Agent placement, for the owner to revise.
    Part keys are saved by position: append only, never reorder.

    Not drawn yet (agent's choice, the art not being made): damage looks per part, the cracked glass, and the overlays of the fits. Not
    given (agent's choice): a walk-in cabin, which waits on the gate the cabins record sets (the Osprey's cabin measured first). Its own
    voice since D'thorga Vehicles 0.127.0 (Sounds_C27J.xml).
  -->
  <Vehicles.VehicleDef ParentName="BaseVehiclePawn">
    <defName>Phobos_C27J</defName>
    <label>C-27J Spartan</label>
    <description>Leonardo C-27J Spartan: a twin-turboprop transport aeroplane. It cannot hover: it takes off along a strip of clear, level ground, rough ground will do, and needs a longer one the heavier it is; it lands on one too. Two pilots fly it; a loadmaster works the ramp; 40 can ride in the hold, or one small vehicle up to 8,600 kg. It drops people, cargo and vehicles by parachute. Unarmoured as built.</description>
